+scientific_hypotheses:
+  - id: PM3-H1
+    title: Additional Arginine Supply and Cerebral Creatine Availability
+    status: Unestablished
+    proposition: >-
+      In an adult experimental model with adequate background glycine and methionine supply, additional oral
+      arginine increases its contribution to cerebral creatine synthesis or delivery and increases cerebral
+      creatine availability compared with an isonitrogenous control.
+    proposed_direction: Additional oral arginine → cerebral creatine contribution and availability
+    rationale: >-
+      Renal precursor-production experiments motivate testing cerebral availability. Existing arginine,
+      glycine and methionine synthesis-capacity admissions do not establish benefit from additional intake.
+    finding_links:
+      supporting: []
+      contradictory_or_null: []
+      contextual:
+        - pm_id: BRS4-FM1-PM3
+          finding_id: PM3-F1
+          href: '#pm3-f1'
+          explanation: Provides bounded pathway context; does not establish the complete hypothesis.
+        - pm_id: BRS4-FM1-PM3
+          finding_id: PM3-F7
+          href: '#pm3-f7'
+          explanation: Provides bounded pathway context; does not establish the complete hypothesis.
+    evidence_sources:
+      - study_id: edison-2007
+        label: Edison et al. (2007)
+        url: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17928413/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: >-
+          Renal GAA production and acute rat arginine/citrulline responses provide precursor context; glycine
+          infusion was null.
+        limitations: Not an oral human brain experiment.
+      - study_id: asiriwardhana-2025
+        label: Asiriwardhana et al. (2025)
+        url: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40998128/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: Dietary methionine influences methyl allocation to products including creatine in neonatal piglets.
+        limitations: Hepatic neonatal context, not adult cerebral delivery.
+      - study_id: solis-2014
+        label: Solis et al. (2014)
+        url: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0007114513003802
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: Vegetarians and omnivores had similar measured brain total creatine despite intake differences.
+        limitations: Cross-sectional; not a direct precursor intervention or refutation of this hypothesis.
+    limitations:
+      - Renal GAA production and neonatal hepatic methyl incorporation are not cerebral delivery measurements.
+      - Arginine infusion is not oral exposure; glycine infusion did not produce the same response.
+      - AGAT feedback, methyl allocation and tissue transport constrain supply.
+      - >-
+        Brain isotope appearance does not distinguish local synthesis from peripheral synthesis and import;
+        greater labelling need not increase the total pool.
+      - No cognitive benefit, effective food dose or new dietary admission is established.
+      - The full 2025 piglet tracer protocol was not independently verified in this appraisal.
+    alternative_explanations:
+      - Peripheral synthesis may rise without greater brain uptake.
+      - >-
+        Turnover or replacement may change without increased pool availability; transport and baseline
+        sufficiency may govern response.
+    required_evidence:
+      - >-
+        Measure oral exposure, peripheral GAA/creatine production, region-specific brain incorporation and
+        total creatine/PCr with precursor-enrichment correction.
+      - >-
+        Support requires increased cerebral contribution and availability; greater labelling alone supports
+        turnover only.
+      - >-
+        A precise cerebral null despite verified exposure and a peripheral response counts against the bounded
+        proposition.
+    provenance:
+      - >-
+        Author-approved bounded hypothesis following the ten-candidate appraisal and individual review on 14
+        November 2026.
+      - >-
+        Approval registers the proposition only. No dietary admissions, SEC, publication gates, food mappings,
+        rankings or accepted graph edges change.
+    review_date: '2026-11-14'
